ve analysis of multimodal RAG system performance, structured as a horizontal layout with three main sections. On the far left, a panel titled 'Multimodel Document' displays a complex, multi-column academic paper page. A specific section of this document, a neural network diagram, is highlighted with a red rectangular outline. An arrow points from this highlighted area to a central panel titled 'Evidence found in the document'. This central panel contains a zoomed-in view of the diagram, labeled 'Figure 3: Word-character level NN architecture for NER'. The diagram illustrates a neural network with layers including 'Char LSTM-F/B', 'Word LSTM-F/B', and 'Bi-CRF', with data flowing from bottom to top, as noted by the text '(The order in the figure is from bottom to top)'. Above this diagram, a question is posed: 'Question: According to Figure 3, what is the sequence followed to generate the final labels for Named Entity Recognition?'. Below the question, the 'Expected answer' is provided: 'The sequence is Characters -> Char Embedding -> Char LSTM-B/F -> Word Features -> Word Representation -> Word LSTM-F/B -> Word CRF-F -> Label.'. The right side of the image is divided into two columns, each presenting the responses of different AI models to the question. The left column shows responses from 'GPT-4o-mini' and 'MIMGraphRAG', both of which incorrectly describe a sequence involving a 'Word CRF layer' or 'word embeddings and character-level convolution' before the LSTM layers. The right column shows responses from 'LightRAG' and 'RAG-Anything'. 'LightRAG' also provides an incorrect answer involving a 'convolution layer'. In contrast, 'RAG-Anything' provides the correct answer: '...involves processing input characters through bi-directional LSTM layers (Char LSTM-B and Char LSTM-F) followed by a softmax or CRF layer that produces the final labels.' Each model's name is followed by a small emoji icon."}
